"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,867\nThe hippocampus as a map\n\n3\n00:00:08,867 --> 00:00:16,669\nIn 1971, John O'Keefe recorded single cells in the hippocampus of rats exploring an open space.\n\n4\n00:00:17,136 --> 00:00:21,037\nOne cell fired only when the rat was in one particular spot.\n\n5\n00:00:21,559 --> 00:00:23,492\nWhen the rat moved somewhere else,\n\n6\n00:00:23,731 --> 00:00:25,072\nthat cell went quiet,\n\n7\n00:00:25,324 --> 00:00:27,419\nand other cells fired instead.\n\n8\n00:00:28,789 --> 00:00:30,420\nHe called them place cells.\n\n9\n00:00:30,741 --> 00:00:33,032\nEach one covers a different patch of space,\n\n10\n00:00:33,205 --> 00:00:34,728\nso together they form a map.\n\n11\n00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:38,852\nKnowing which cells are active tells you where the animal is.\n\n12\n00:00:39,227 --> 00:00:45,209\nIn 2014, O'Keefe shared the Nobel Prize with May-Britt and Edvard Moser,\n\n13\n00:00:45,453 --> 00:00:48,129\nwho found grid cells in a neighbouring region.\n\n14\n00:00:49,299 --> 00:00:51,470\nDo people use the hippocampus as a map?\n\n15\n00:00:51,892 --> 00:00:58,173\nTo qualify, London taxi drivers spend three to four years learning about 25,000 streets.\n\n16\n00:00:58,589 --> 00:01:03,323\nEleanor Maguire and Katherine Woollett scanned 79 trainees before and after.\n\n17\n00:01:03,807 --> 00:01:09,035\nThe 39 who qualified had more grey matter at the back of the hippocampus than when they started.\n\n18\n00:01:09,598 --> 00:01:13,806\nThose who failed, and people who never trained,\n\n19\n00:01:13,894 --> 00:01:14,174\nshowed no such change.\n\n20\n00:01:15,944 --> 00:01:18,041\nThe map can hold more than streets.\n\n21\n00:01:18,352 --> 00:01:19,776\nIn the method of loci,\n\n22\n00:01:19,809 --> 00:01:23,123\nyou place things you want to remember along a route you know well,\n\n23\n00:01:23,366 --> 00:01:25,838\nthen walk the route in your mind to recall them.\n\n24\n00:01:26,348 --> 00:01:28,410\nMemory champions use this method.\n\n25\n00:01:28,865 --> 00:01:31,886\nBrain scans showed they were no smarter than others,\n\n26\n00:01:32,063 --> 00:01:33,902\nand their brains were built the same.\n\n27\n00:01:34,476 --> 00:01:37,238\nWhat differed was that they used spatial memory,\n\n28\n00:01:37,383 --> 00:01:39,107\nincluding the hippocampus.\n\n29\n00:01:40,477 --> 00:01:41,709\nCan anyone learn it?\n\n30\n00:01:42,173 --> 00:01:44,214\nIn a 2017 study,\n\n31\n00:01:44,429 --> 00:01:48,255\npeople with ordinary memories practised the method for six weeks.\n\n32\n00:01:48,666 --> 00:01:53,760\nTheir recall of a list of 72 words rose from 26 to 62,\n\n33\n00:01:53,984 --> 00:01:56,268\nand four months later it was still high.\n\n34\n00:01:57,638 --> 00:01:59,446\nWhy does this matter for learning?\n\n35\n00:01:59,852 --> 00:02:03,028\nHang new information on a structure you already know,\n\n36\n00:02:03,161 --> 00:02:04,504\nsuch as a familiar route,\n\n37\n00:02:04,524 --> 00:02:06,383\nand practise walking through it.\n\n38\n00:02:06,784 --> 00:02:09,182\nAnd expect deep learning to take time.\n\n39\n00:02:09,666 --> 00:02:12,079\nThe taxi drivers needed years.\n\n40\n00:02:13,849 --> 00:02:16,447\nThe hippocampus maps the places you know.\n\n41\n00:02:16,857 --> 00:02:20,122\nYou can use that map to remember much more than places.\n\n"